ical of the  government according to a media report  the government felt  these posts constituted social media  misuse  and for more on this i'm joined now by  pratik sinha he's the co-founder of alt  news  a fact checking portal in india pratik  joins me now  from ahmedabad in western india prateek  you're focused on a daily basis  on reading out fake news and  misinformation online  in india did most of the tweets that  have been blocked  meet that standard not at all there was  no misinformation  these are people expressing their  opinions about the ground situation for  example  one act of anith kumar singh saying that  it is difficult to get medication  another  uh you know member of parliament from  another political party saying that you  know the prime minister should be held  responsible  uh a journalist a senior journalist from  an organization called abp news  saying that you know you criticized the  chamath incident you know which happened  where the muslim community was  criticized for holding a religious  congregation but you're not doing the  same for  hindus who are who held a similar  religious congregation in something  called kummela  so these were all very genuine criticism  with no element of misinformation or  hate and  does not at all meet the standards of  twitter as a platform to police these  tweets  so is the government then using its  powers  to block dissent on social media  absolutely all of these are tweets which  would be considered critical of the  government  and the government is uh forcing  platforms and unfortunately the  platforms are buckling as well  but the government is forcing platforms  to adhere to certain lines where  you know where descent is curved  now you mentioned the platforms are  buckling to the government's  instructions why  because in india if you have to do  business you have to be  friends with the government if we keep  talking about ease of business but  it is ease of business for friends and  we have seen this in other platforms  also  even on facebook there are individuals  working in very high positions who  at one point of time used to do  propaganda for bjp so  uh this is not a case of just one  platform all platforms  in india social media platforms want to  do business india is a huge market for  them  and for them to be successful they have  to be friends with the government  what exactly are the legal powers that  the government of india has  that allow it to ask social media  platforms to remove  content deemed objectionable  so everything is not in a legal realm  right  there can be various things we have seen  in india that  um you know for example opposition  political parties or  media organizations recently they are  subjected to  uh income tax rates raised by the  enforcement directorate and things like  that so  uh government can uh pressurize business  houses in multiple ways you know  whether it's permission for a new  building new land  there are multiple ways in which a  government can block the progress  of a business it is not only in legal  realms or you know through  though they have also used those kind of  threats that is  you know if you don't take down these  set of tweets in the past when there was  another  problematic hashtag that was trended  government you did use  legal threats as well but that is not  the government has more means than that  to  intimidate business houses but overall  our social media platforms  helping in the current crisis i mean the  number of tweets that we have seen for  example you have citizens  coordinating and organizing amongst  themselves  trying to find responses to this  nationwide crisis  is that a major positive to come out of  this  it is but these are two distinct issues  uh definitely  uh twitter especially has been a huge  platform  uh for you know this kind of  crowdsourcing resources and all news as  a not-for-profit organization  every month we do crowdfunding and  twitter is one of our biggest platform  to be able to do that and that is the  nature of the platform and  we are appreciated appreciative of that  but but that  that is a very distinct matter from uh  the policing  or from their inability to take down  hate speech  there are multiple times when we have  written to twitter about accounts which  have been suspended which come back and  continue to put out hate speech and  which is not which is against the  policies and they don't act at all  so these are two distinct possibilities  uh distinct issues that  you know a platform being useful and how  a platform  is buckling under government pressure  speaking of buckling under  government pressure do you see in the  midst of the present coronavirus crisis  is there actually an increase in  misleading information and deliberate  fake news that is being spread  absolutely  there's a huge increase in both medical  misinformation  as well as political misinformation the  last one year has been just  in you know endless amounts of uh  political and  medical misinformation political  misinformation is very closely linked in  india to current affairs  and it is organized that is it is used  to set narratives so  and a lot of it comes from the  right-wing government so if things are  going wrong  then false narratives are created using  social media  so with and it is done in a very  organized manner and we have seen a huge  uptick of it pratik sanako founder of  old news thank you so much
 

